1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,320
Ever get a sharp twinge on one side of your lower belly, right in the middle of your cycle, and it's not even close to your period?

2
00:00:06,640 --> 00:00:14,560
That's ovulation pain, and it affects more than 40% of women of reproductive age — for many of them, it happens almost every single month.

3
00:00:14,880 --> 00:00:16,720
Here's what's going on inside your body.

4
00:00:17,040 --> 00:00:21,440
Right before an egg is released, the growing follicle stretches the surface of the ovary.

5
00:00:21,760 --> 00:00:26,560
Then, when it ruptures, the fluid it releases irritates the lining of your abdomen.

6
00:00:26,880 --> 00:00:31,920
Prostaglandins — the same compounds involved in period cramps — drive both of those steps.

7
00:00:32,240 --> 00:00:40,720
The pain is usually felt on just one side, matching whichever ovary released the egg that month, and it can last anywhere from a few minutes to about 48 hours.

8
00:00:41,040 --> 00:00:43,600
There are two simple things that help most people.

9
00:00:43,920 --> 00:00:50,400
First, heat — a heating pad or hot water bottle on your lower belly relaxes the muscles and takes the edge off fast.

10
00:00:50,720 --> 00:00:57,120
Second, an NSAID like ibuprofen taken at the first sign of pain blocks the same prostaglandins that are causing it.

11
00:00:57,440 --> 00:00:59,280
It also helps to track your cycle.

12
00:00:59,600 --> 00:01:06,320
Mark the days you expect ovulation on a calendar or period app, so you can keep pain relief on hand before the ache even starts.

13
00:01:06,640 --> 00:01:09,680
That said, this isn't always just ovulation pain.

14
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:15,520
See a doctor if the pain lasts more than 48 hours, or if it comes with nausea, vomiting, or dizziness.

15
00:01:15,840 --> 00:01:25,760
Sudden, severe pain on one side needs urgent care — it could be a sign of ovarian torsion, an ectopic pregnancy, or a ruptured cyst, and none of those should wait.
